purpose='Ceritinib blocks abnormal ALK signals that help some lung cancers grow. In advanced cancer after crizotinib, the aim is to control cancer and delay progression. The choice depends on your tumour test, previous treatment, other options and priorities.'
scope='Adults with ALK-positive advanced non-small-cell lung cancer previously treated with crizotinib: NCCP 00340a. This suite does not turn the broader first-line licence into an NCCP indication or funding approval. Confirm your treatment setting with the team.'
schedule='The usual starting dose is 450 mg once daily with food, every day without a planned rest week. Follow your own prescription. Treatment continues while it helps and side effects remain acceptable; there is no fixed course length.'
administration='Take at the same time daily with food. Swallow capsules whole with water; do not chew or crush. Take a missed dose unless the next is due within 12 hours; ask your pharmacist if timing is unclear. Never double a dose. If you vomit, do not replace the dose; take the next at its usual time.'
food='If illness prevents you eating with the dose, contact the team. Only the prescriber can arrange a different fasting dose. Do not alternate between fed and fasting schedules or change the dose yourself.'
monitor='Before starting: ALK test, blood count, kidney and liver tests, pancreatic enzymes, fasting glucose, ECG and symptom/medicine review. Blood count, kidneys and fasting glucose every 4 weeks; liver tests every 2 weeks for the first 3 months, then monthly. ECG after 1 month, then as needed. Extra heart, electrolyte, glucose or pancreatic tests may be needed for risks, symptoms or abnormal results; scans follow the consultant plan.'
urgent='Contact oncology urgently for new or worsening cough or breathlessness, fainting, palpitations, severe dizziness, yellow skin or eyes, dark urine, severe persistent abdominal pain, persistent vomiting or diarrhoea, poor drinking or little urine, or marked thirst with frequent urination and feeling unwell. Severe breathing difficulty, collapse or severe chest pain needs emergency help.'
anti='Ceritinib has moderate-to-high oral sickness risk. Ask for daily prevention and rescue instructions. Your team must check heart-rhythm and medicine interactions when choosing anti-sickness treatment; do not add medicines or a steroid course yourself.'
interactions='Avoid grapefruit or grapefruit juice and St John’s wort. Check prescription, pharmacy and herbal medicines before use, including heart medicines, antibiotics, antifungals, seizure treatments and acid-reducing medicines. Do not stop a prescribed medicine without advice.'
repro='Discuss pregnancy, fertility and breastfeeding before treatment. Women who could become pregnant need highly effective contraception during treatment and for 3 months afterwards; hormonal contraception may be less effective, so agree a suitable method. Do not breastfeed during this treatment. Fertility effects are uncertain; ask about preservation if relevant.'
evidence_patient='A study after crizotinib and platinum chemotherapy found longer disease control with ceritinib than the chemotherapy used at that time. It tested an older fasting dose. A separate dose study supports taking the current lower dose with food; its main efficacy group had not had previous treatment. These studies cannot predict your outcome or rank all current treatment options.'
